    }
}

lessons.theory:
## Talking to C (FFI)
Rust doesn't exist in a vacuum. The world runs on C. Operating systems, database engines (SQLite), and encryption libraries (OpenSSL) expose C APIs. 

A **Foreign Function Interface (FFI)** allows Rust to call C code, and C to call Rust code, with zero performance overhead.

```rust
extern "C" {
    // Tell Rust this C function exists in an external library
    fn abs(input: i32) -> i32;
}

fn main() {
    unsafe {
        // Calling C code is inherently unsafe!
        println!("Absolute value of -3 according to C is: {}", abs(-3));
    }
}
```
This allows companies to slowly rewrite massive, buggy C++ codebases by replacing them one function at a time with safe Rust code, compiling it down to a standard C-compatible binary.

