## OS-Level Concurrency
Unlike Go (which uses lightweight virtual goroutines), Rust's `std::thread::spawn` maps directly 1-to-1 to actual Operating System threads.

OS threads are heavier to create, but they offer maximum performance and control. Because they map directly to hardware threads on your CPU core, they are ideal for CPU-bound tasks like heavy mathematical computations, video encoding, or game engines.

```rust
use std::thread;

// Spawn a new OS thread
let handle = thread::spawn(|| {
    // Heavy computation here
});
```
When you spawn a thread, the OS scheduler decides when it gets CPU time. Because threads share the same physical memory space, coordinating them is the hardest part of systems programming.

